            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a firebrand, then the Latin for burning heat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: cremō, cremāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cremō, cremāre — \"to burn up, to cremate\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ignis tōtum vīcum cremāvit. The fire burned up the whole street."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cremō, cremāre, cremāvī, cremātum. Romans usually burned their dead, and English cremate comes from this verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cremō is inherited; EtymDB traces it to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to burn\", also seen in carbō, \"coal\". English cremate comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
